INT. DRIVE-IN PROJECTION BOOTH - NIGHT

A cramped brick box. An old carbon-arc projector clatters. Film cans stacked like pizza boxes. Through the slit window, a giant screen glows over an empty lot.

RAFAELA (17, denim jacket, a lighter in her fist) sits on the stool. JUNE (17, cardigan, hair pinned back) stands at the door. Both are tense.

JUNE
You told me it was just a smoke.

RAFAELA
It is just a smoke.

She flicks the lighter. A small flame. She stares at it, then at the stacked cans.

JUNE
That's nitrate film, Rafaela.

RAFAELA
I know.

JUNE
It burns like gasoline.

She clicks the lighter shut. Too late. A can on the shelf flares with a quiet whump.

JUNE (CONT'D)
Oh my God.

Smoke blooms. She grabs a blanket and smothers it. Rafaela stamps. They cough, eyes wide, bodies bent.

The door swings open. MR. KOWALCZYK (60s, suspenders, a flashlight) stands there. Smoke curls around his head.

MR. KOWALCZYK
Which one of you is on fire?

RAFAELA
Nobody. It's the projector.

MR. KOWALCZYK
The projector is not made of smoke.

JUNE
It was an accident, sir.

He sniffs. Looks at the blackened can. Then at the two of them. For a long moment, he says nothing.

MR. KOWALCZYK
That was the last reel of "The Birds."

RAFAELA
We're so sorry.

MR. KOWALCZYK
It was a bad print anyway.

He tosses the flashlight to June. She catches it.

MR. KOWALCZYK (CONT'D)
Open a window. And never bring fire in here again.

He leaves. They stare at each other. Then they laugh, shaky and helpless, as the screen outside goes dark.
